## What Does the Average Nail Appointment Actually Cost?

A simple question deserves a simple answer, except this one only looks simple. <strong>How much do nails cost</strong> for an average appointment? Most US salon visits land between $35 and $75, covering everything from a basic gel manicure to a mid-range acrylic set. That range holds across most of the country, with city salons pushing toward the top and rural salons sitting closer to the bottom.

The "average nail appointment" is a moving target because it depends which service is doing the work. A polish change takes fifteen minutes and costs $15 to $20. A full acrylic set with shaping can run ninety minutes and cost $80 or more. Both are "a nail appointment." Neither is the average.

What holds steady is the structure underneath the price. Every nail service is built from the same components: nail prep, product, application time, and whatever the salon charges for the room you're sitting in. A salon in a strip mall and a boutique studio in a converted brownstone can use identical gel polish and technique, yet charge $30 apart, because rent and reputation are baked into the second number as much as the product is.

There's a second layer worth naming here, because no competitor pricing guide currently covers it: 2026 has brought a measurable shift in product costs. Tariff pressure on imported gel systems, acrylic powders, and UV lamps pushed several major suppliers to raise wholesale prices through the first half of the year, and salons have passed a portion of that increase to clients. If your gel manicure costs five to ten dollars more than it did at the start of 2025, that's not your salon overcharging you. It's a supply chain story playing out one appointment at a time.

One more thing worth knowing before you walk in: almost every price quoted online is a starting price. "Gel manicure from $35" means $35 covers the simplest version. Length, shape, removal of a previous set, and any design work sit on top of that floor.

